From b8df9a72aeddeee7fc7d429419bae72922ac2d7e Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Fountain5405 <Fountain5405@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Tue, 10 Nov 2026 04:23:04 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 1/1] multiexp: constant-time multiexp uses Straus with a 4-bit
 window at every length

multiexp() is the constant-time function. It picked its algorithm and window
from algorithm(len), the table that multiexp_vartime() also uses. In release
builds that is Straus with windows 3-5 below 50 pairs, then Pippenger with
windows 4-8 (with debug assertions, Straus up to 99 pairs). Those choices
suit the variable-time functions. On Selene points the variable-time choice
was within 0-5% of the best at every length measured (16 to 2,048 pairs).
They do not suit the constant-time functions:
- To hide which bucket each digit selects, constant-time Pippenger reads and
  writes all 2^window buckets for every pair and digit. So its cost grows
  with the window instead of falling.
- Its best window was 3 or 4 at every length measured, against the table's
  6-8 from 125 pairs.
- Constant-time Straus with a 4-bit window was as fast as or faster than
  constant-time Pippenger at its best window, at every length measured.

Why the crate chose these windows is not known. Its comment says they came
from benchmarking k256 and curve25519-dalek. Its benchmark (src/tests,
ignored) times the constant-time straus() and pippenger(), and raises the
window within each. It never compares the two.

The change:
- Two or more pairs now always use the crate's constant-time straus() with
  a 4-bit window. Zero and one pair keep their special cases. The choice
  depends only on the number of pairs, as before.
- straus() and pippenger() are unchanged. pippenger() is no longer called
  outside the tests and the benchmark, so outside tests it is marked
  allow(dead_code).
- multiexp_vartime() is unchanged.
- No new constant-time code: existing constant-time code runs at more
  lengths.

Where it applies in monerodato:
- In fcmp_pp_rust, multiexp() runs only in the FCMP++ membership prover:
  generalized-bulletproofs ArithmeticCircuitStatement::prove, and
  full-chain-membership-proofs Fcmp::prove with its vector-commitment tape.
- It runs on Selene and Helios points only. monerod and monero-wallet-rpc of
  monerodato.2 have no other instance of it, so ed25519's preference for a
  3-bit window (about 6% faster than 4 there) does not apply.
- monerod does not reach it. In the release monerod:
  - its only callers are those two provers;
  - they are reached only from fcmp_pp::prove_membership;
  - nothing in monerod calls that function, or loads its address. Its one
    caller is src/wallet/tx_builder.cpp.
- The verifiers, the curve-tree hashing and the sync all use
  multiexp_vartime().
- So this changes how the wallet builds transactions, and nothing in the
  daemon.

Speed of multiexp(), constant time, one core of a 12-core Ryzen 9 3900X (16 GB RAM, SATA SSD), median of
41 runs. Each figure is the 0.5.2 choice's time divided by Straus with a
4-bit window:
  pairs     4     8    16    32    64   128   256   512  1024  2048
  Selene  1.06  1.08  1.00  1.14  1.31  1.77  1.55  3.22  3.06  2.98
  Helios  1.06  1.06  1.00  1.17  1.37  1.70  1.50  2.82  2.66  2.58
At 16 pairs both run Straus with a 4-bit window.

Transactions built by monero-wallet-rpc (sweep_all, not relayed) from a
copy of a stressnet wallet, against an offline monerod, on the same machine
with the performance governor. Both wallet-rpc builds are ARCH=native;
before is monerodato.2, after is this change. 3 rounds, alternating which
build ran first. Median wall time per transaction (range):
  inputs  before                    after                    after/before
       8    6.35 s (6.35-7.31)        3.11 s (3.06-3.93)     0.49
      32   24.68 s (24.65-25.09)     12.08 s (12.02-12.30)    0.49
     128  100.83 s (98.99-101.02)    52.09 s (51.27-53.28)    0.52
Fee, weight and size were identical before and after, at each input count.
Peak resident memory of each wallet-rpc process over all its runs (VmHWM):
1,311,100 kB before, 1,312,548 kB after.

Tests:
- New, src/tests/constant_time_straus.rs:
  - multiexp() against the 0.5.2 dispatch (both of its tables, with and
    without debug assertions), multiexp_vartime(), straus(_, 4) and the naive
    sum;
  - lengths on either side of every threshold (9/10 to 749/750), plus 2-5,
    1,000 and 1,024 pairs;
  - on secp256k1 and ed25519, with the edge scalars 0, 1 and -1 and the
    identity included.
  A deliberately wrong result at 275 pairs fails it.
- The crate's tests pass, debug and release, and the crate builds with
  alloc only (no std).

Proofs: a fixed-seed differential, in a harness outside this tree. The
fcmps crate's tests were built twice from the same patched/monero-oxide,
once against this multiexp and once against crates.io 0.5.2, with OsRng
replaced by a seeded generator so that proving is deterministic. 258 cases:
1 to 128 inputs, 1 to 8 layers, the crate's random paths and full trees
with one leaf chunk per input.
- All 258 proofs are byte-identical between the two builds.
- The 0.5.2 build verified every proof the patched build made; each build
  also verified its own.
- 50 cases proved again on the 0.5.2 build gave the same bytes.
- Prove time in that harness (10 cases at a time, so indicative only): the
  patched build's median time over 0.5.2's was 0.41-0.49 from 2 to 128
  inputs, and 0.72 at 1 input.

Other tests, with this multiexp:
- generalized-bulletproofs, full-chain-membership-proofs and
  monero-fcmp-plus-plus crate tests, release: 26 passed;
- this tree's unit tests for fcmp_pp, wallet_tx_builder, carrot_fcmp,
  curve_trees and bulletproofs_plus: 41 passed.

